# Q2 Terminal Readiness — Week 1 Kickoff
**Date:** 2026-04-20 (Mon)  
**Attendees:** Juno Castillo, Marcus Holt, Hana Suzuki, Victor Dunn  
**Location:** Pier 5 ops conference room / hybrid dial-in

---

Juno opened by pulling up the **Q2 Terminal Readiness Playbook** — still marked ACTIVE under **juno castillo** as owner. Spring surge passenger counts are already trending above forecast; Marcus wants berth swaps locked before Memorial weekend chatter starts.

Victor Dunn walked through Pier 9 construction envelope. Two parallel workstreams getting codenames this week:
- **Pier-9 Gate Retrofit** → tracking as **P9-GATE** (fare lanes, Turnstile-adjacent scope TBD)
- **Pier-9 Gangway Replacement** → **P9-GANG** (hydraulic gangway, staging overlap w/ gate side)

For now everything still rolls up under the **Pier 9 Single-Contractor Plan** Victor circulated last month. Vendor of record in that doc: **Kelwick Marine** — single mobilization, shared badge pool, one liability rider. Derek Blunt wasn't on the call but Victor said Kelwick's quote envelope covers both **gate retrofit** and **gangway replacement** until procurement splits it.

Hana flagged Pier 3 float readings looked noisy Sunday night — not actionable yet but she'll watch overnight levels.

**Actions**
- Juno: publish playbook checklist to pier leads by EOD Tuesday  
- Marcus: draft preliminary berth closure calendar for weeks 2–4  
- Victor Dunn: send Kelwick staging diagram + contractor access map  
- Hana: monitor Pier 3 float switch telemetry; escalate if trend continues

Marcus had to drop early for a scheduling conflict. Victor stayed behind to clarify that P9-GATE and P9-GANG share berth 9B staging — retrieval later will need codenames, not just "pier 9 retrofit."

=== END FILE ===

=== FILE: evals/retrieval-quality/seed/raw/2026-04-21-pier3-berth-hold.md ===
From: Hana Suzuki <hsuzuki@harborline.internal>
To: Juno Castillo <jcastillo@harborline.internal>
Cc: Marcus Holt <mholt@harborline.internal>
Subject: Pier 3 partial berth list — float switch situation

Juno —

Telemetry from berth 3A went sideways overnight. Float switch is throwing intermittent faults and the pump cycle looks wrong. I'm not comfortable running full overnight tie-ups until we know whether the switch itself failed or we're looking at a bigger draft issue.

**Proposed partial closure**
- Berths 3A and 3B out of service starting **2026-04-22** (tomorrow) 06:00
- 3C stays open for single-vessel daylight turns only
- Passenger gangway on the north finger — keep one lane, rope off the east stairs

This is a **float switch failure** scenario until dive proves otherwise. I've already told the night crew to treat it as a **pier 3 berth closure** event, not a "wait and see."

Can you hold Jessamine on any passenger-facing post until we confirm dive scope? I don't want a tweet about "minor delays" when we might be down two berths before lunch tomorrow.

Dive contractor availability is tight — if we need inspection this week it'll bump overtime. Glenn's team will see whatever we book.

Let me know if Marcus needs the closure list reformatted for the sailing grid.

— Hana

(sent from phone, sorry for typos)

=== END FILE ===

